---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const meta = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'UX Research & Design (solo)' },
  { label: 'Timeline', value: '12–13 weeks, 2024' },
  { label: 'Context', value: 'Swinburne capstone with Penfolds / Treasury Wine Estates' },
];

const personas = [
  {
    name: 'Sarah',
    trait: 'Mobile-first shopper',
    detail:
      'Browses and buys almost entirely on her phone. Values sites that clearly work on mobile rather than tolerate it.',
    goals: [
      'Complete a purchase on a phone without friction',
      'See the real cost early — no surprise tax or shipping at the last step',
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'James',
    trait: 'Confident across devices',
    detail:
      'Comfortable with technology, moves between laptop and phone mid-purchase. Cares about who he is buying from.',
    goals: [
      'Buy quickly and know the transaction is secure',
      'Confirm the retailer is local, with a returns policy he can find',
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Penfolds — Navigation and Checkout Redesign · Josh Illichmann"
  description="Case study: a UX research and design project rethinking global navigation, the mini-cart and checkout for Penfolds.com. Swinburne capstone with Treasury Wine Estates."
>
  <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 overflow-x-hidden">
    <div class="space-y-12 py-12 animate-fade-in-up">
      <!-- Back link -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <a
          href="/"
          class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-600 hover:text-gray-900 transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2"
        >
          ← BACK
        </a>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-600 uppercase">Case Study</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Hero -->
      <header class="space-y-6">
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-medium font-mono leading-tight">
          Penfolds — Navigation and Checkout Redesign
        </h1>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          A heritage wine brand with a global catalogue, a navigation menu that had
          grown to hold all of it, and a checkout that asked people to create an
          account before they could buy.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500">
          A Swinburne University capstone, run with the Penfolds team at Treasury
          Wine Estates.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6 pt-4 text-sm">
          {
            meta.map((item) => (
              <div class="space-y-1">
                <h2 class="font-medium uppercase font-mono text-xs">{item.label}</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-600">{item.value}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </header>
    </div>

    <!-- Context -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">CONTEXT</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-4 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            This was my capstone project as a UX design student at Swinburne
            University, run with Penfolds — part of Treasury Wine Estates, where I
            had previously worked. It ran for about 12 to 13 weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            It was not a paid engagement, and I want to be straight about that. What
            it was: a real brief, on a real commercial site, with the Penfolds team
            sharing their Google Analytics data and sitting through the presentation
            at the end. The constraints were real even if the contract wasn&apos;t.
          </p>
          <p>
            The scope was three connected surfaces — the global navigation, the
            mini-cart, and the checkout.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Problem -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">THE PROBLEM</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-4 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Penfolds sells a large, deep catalogue — wines by range, by region, by
            variety, by vintage, plus cellar doors, restaurants, events, clubs and a
            century of brand history. All of it had found its way into the global
            navigation.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result was a menu where categories expanded into more categories,
            which expanded again. Every item that went in made the menu more
            complete and every item made it harder to use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Four problems compounded:</span>{' '}
            cognitive overload from too many simultaneous options; a cluttered
            presentation working against a brand built on restraint; a structure
            that fought the mobile devices most people were arriving on; and reduced
            discoverability, where important pages were harder to find precisely
            because so much surrounded them.
          </p>
          <p>
            The mini-cart and checkout had the second half of the problem. A
            multi-step checkout, limited payment options, no visible signals that
            the transaction was secure, and mandatory account creation before
            purchase. Each one is a reason to leave a cart behind.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Research -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">RESEARCH</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I ran interviews and surveys with people from a range of backgrounds,
            looking at how they browse and buy online generally — not only on
            Penfolds. Alongside that: competitive analysis, published case studies,
            and the Google Analytics data Penfolds provided for their own site.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">What came back consistently</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>Most people were shopping on mobile devices.</li>
              <li>
                Bloated, confusing navigation was actively disliked — and named
                unprompted.
              </li>
              <li>
                Search matters disproportionately on mobile, and people expect to
                find it immediately.
              </li>
              <li>
                Payment choice matters. Afterpay, PayPal, Apple Pay, Google Pay, or
                simply knowing their card is accepted.
              </li>
              <li>
                Poor mobile responsiveness was enough on its own to end a session.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>
            The analytics gave me the other half: which parts of the site people
            actually used. That turned navigation from a matter of taste into a
            matter of evidence.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Users -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">TARGET USERS</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6">
          <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            Two personas came out of the interviews, and I designed against their
            goals rather than their demographics.
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
            {
              personas.map((p) => (
                <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 space-y-3">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="font-medium">{p.name}</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500">{p.trait}</p>
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{p.detail}</p>
                  <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-1 list-disc pl-5">
                    {p.goals.map((g) => <li>{g}</li>)}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Navigation -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">NAVIGATION</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I re-mapped and re-prioritised the menu against the analytics rather
            than against the org chart. Five top-level items survived: Grange, Our
            Wines, Experiences, Our Story, and Login.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The decisions</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">Grange sits first.</span> It is the wine
                the brand is known for and the data agreed.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">Our Wines collapsed to four entries</span>{' '}
                — New Arrivals, Featured, Limited Editions, All Wines. The granular
                cuts people actually browse by, like variety and region, moved out of
                the menu.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">
                  Granular categories became pre-filtered views, linked from the
                  footer.
                </span>{' '}
                This is the part I am happiest with. Region and variety pages did not
                get deleted — they became filtered states of the wine listing, reached
                from the footer. The menu got shorter without the site losing a single
                route.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">Experiences stayed prominent</span> —
                Cellar Doors, Restaurants, Events — because usage justified it, not
                because it felt important.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="text-gray-900">Our Story kept History, Winemaking and Awards</span>{' '}
                at top level; sustainability, philosophy and the story of Grange moved
                to the footer.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>
            The footer stopped being a dumping ground and became the second half of
            the information architecture — the place for depth, once the menu was
            handling breadth.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="pb-12 bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
      <img
        src="/penfolds/nav-desktop.png"
        alt="Redesigned Penfolds global navigation with each top-level menu expanded: Grange, Our Wines, Experiences, Our Story and Login"
        width="1343"
        height="1600"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        class="w-full h-auto"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- Mini-cart -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">THE MINI-CART</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
              The existing cart used an X to close the panel and an X to remove an
              item.
            </span>{' '}
            Two identical controls, inches apart, with very different consequences —
            one dismisses the cart, the other deletes something you chose to buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            I replaced the destructive one with the word <em>Remove</em>. The X now
            means only one thing. It is a small change and it is the one I would
            defend hardest: the cost of getting it wrong is a customer losing a
            selection they had already committed to.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The rest of the panel</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                Subtotal shown immediately, with shipping explicitly noted as
                calculated at checkout — no surprise at the final step.
              </li>
              <li>
                A quantity dropdown that starts at one and cannot reach zero.
                Removing an item is a deliberate act, not an accident of decrementing.
              </li>
              <li>
                Visual hierarchy following how the eye moves: image, then name, then
                format and price, then quantity, with Remove last — present, but never
                the first thing found.
              </li>
              <li>
                A prominent checkout button carrying a padlock, and a quieter
                continue-shopping route for people not finished yet.
              </li>
              <li>Suggested products kept, below the decision rather than above it.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="pb-12 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
        <img
          src="/penfolds/minicart-desktop.png"
          alt="Redesigned mini-cart with subtotal, quantity dropdowns and a Remove text link"
          width="364"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Checkout -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">CHECKOUT</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
              The significant product decision was removing the requirement to create
              an account before buying.
            </span>
          </p>
          <p>
            The business case for forced registration is real: an account captures an
            email address, supports marketing, and builds a customer profile. It is
            not an irrational thing to want. But published research on checkout
            abandonment puts the cost of compelling it at roughly a quarter of
            would-be purchasers, and a customer who leaves gives you neither the sale
            nor the email address.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the account became optional, and the newsletter became a checkbox
            inside the flow. The business still gets the address from people happy to
            give it, and stops trading sales for the ones who are not.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Supporting decisions</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
              <li>
                Express payment via Apple Pay and PayPal, with room for others.
                Familiar payment branding does double duty as a trust signal.
              </li>
              <li>
                Fewer fields: address autocomplete instead of a column of inputs,
                and billing defaulting to the shipping address.
              </li>
              <li>
                A padlock at the point of payment. Most people never look at the
                browser address bar for the SSL indicator, so the reassurance has to
                live where the decision is made.
              </li>
              <li>
                A two-column split — inputs on the left, order summary on the right —
                so what you are buying stays visible while you enter details.
              </li>
              <li>
                Free-shipping threshold surfaced in the summary, and a confirmation
                page with order number, shipping and payment details.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Mobile -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">MOBILE</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-6 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Mobile was not a narrower version of the desktop design. Two decisions
            diverged deliberately.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">Search moved to the top of the menu.</span>{' '}
            Interviews were unambiguous that search carries more weight on a phone, so
            opening the hamburger puts search first, expanding into a dedicated field
            and button rather than sending people to a separate page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">The mini-cart overlay disappeared entirely.</span>{' '}
            On a small screen, an overlay summarising a cart before a separate
            checkout is a step that costs more than it gives. Tapping the cart goes
            straight to checkout, with the order summary collapsed behind a
            show-full-summary control for anyone who wants to verify it. Same
            information, one less stop.
          </p>
          <p>
            The footer restructured from five columns to two, and the nav headings
            took extra weight to separate parent from child in a stacked list.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="pb-12 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
        <img
          src="/penfolds/home-mobile.png"
          alt="Penfolds mobile homepage"
          width="393"
          height="854"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
        <img
          src="/penfolds/nav-mobile.png"
          alt="Mobile navigation open, with Search elevated above every other item"
          width="393"
          height="852"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
        <img
          src="/penfolds/checkout-mobile.png"
          alt="Mobile checkout with collapsible order summary and express payment"
          width="393"
          height="852"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Accessibility -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">ACCESSIBILITY</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-4 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Accessibility was set as a project goal rather than a final pass: colour
            contrast, touch targets sized for real thumbs, correct labelling, and an
            information order that still makes sense read aloud by a screen reader.
          </p>
          <p>
            Consistency between desktop and mobile did related work — the same
            elements in the same relative places, so moving between devices
            mid-purchase does not mean relearning the interface.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-900">
            Honestly: this was specified and designed for, not audited. I did not run
            it against assistive technology, and I would not claim it as verified.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What happened -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">WHAT HAPPENED</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10 space-y-4 text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I built interactive prototypes for desktop and mobile in Figma, matching
            the existing Penfolds design language, and presented the work back to
            their team with the prototypes shared for them to use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">
              Then it stopped. The designs were never implemented.
            </span>
          </p>
          <p>
            Validation testing was the next step and the project ended before it ran,
            so I have no usability findings and no conversion numbers. I would rather
            say that plainly than dress up a prediction as a result.
          </p>
          <p>
            As for why none of it shipped — it was not a verdict on the work. Changes
            to a site like this move through commercial, brand, legal, technical and
            regional approval gates, and a redesign of the global navigation touches
            every one of them. The work was sound and it had no route through the
            organisation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium">That is the part I actually took with me.</span>{' '}
            I spent eight years in change management before this, and I had watched
            good proposals die in approval chains from the other side of the table.
            Penfolds was the first time it happened to something I had designed
            myself. A design that cannot be adopted is not finished, and adoption is a
            design problem — it needs a route mapped through the organisation, and
            stakeholders brought along early enough to have a stake in it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If I ran this again, I would spend less time perfecting the prototype and
            more time on the smallest shippable slice — the mini-cart X, say — and on
            who would need to say yes to it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Testimonial -->
    <section class="py-12">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-8 md:gap-14">
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-2">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-mono">FEEDBACK</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="col-span-1 md:col-span-10">
          <blockquote class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 space-y-4">
            <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              &ldquo;This is a much cleaner and user-friendly experience through these
              key areas of the site. The navigation, mini cart, and checkout
              improvements are fantastic, especially for mobile users. Honestly
              brilliant work.&rdquo;
            </p>
            <footer class="text-sm text-gray-600">
              <span class="text-gray-900">Grace Burke</span> — Global Website
              Experience Manager, Penfolds (Treasury Wine Estates)
            </footer>
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- More projects -->
    <section class="py-12 border-t border-gray-200">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-mono mb-6">MORE PROJECTS</h2>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-3">
        <a
          href="/gigz"
          class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-800 hover:text-gray-600 transition-colors"
        >
          GIGZ →
        </a>
        <a
          href="/convertiq"
          class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-800 hover:text-gray-600 transition-colors"
        >
          CONVERTIQ →
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="py-8 text-sm text-gray-500 font-mono">
      <p>© {new Date().getFullYear()}</p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
